            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The primary consensus interprets a mountain as a place of divine presence, transcendent revelation, and encounter (e.g., Sinai, Zion, the Transfiguration), while widely acknowledging a secondary prophetic meaning of kingdoms, empires, or governmental powers.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines a mountain as a kingdom, nation, or high place, specifically representing the seat of ruling authority.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A location of divine encounter, revelation, and God's presence.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A kingdom, nation, or seat of ruling authority.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book elevates a recognized secondary consensus meaning (kingdoms/powers) to its sole definition, omitting the primary consensus category of divine presence, revelation, and worship.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus identifies the central symbolism of mountains as theophany and proximity to God, whereas the book defines them strictly in terms of political or ruling authority.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By defining a mountain solely as a kingdom or seat of authority, the book completely replaces the primary consensus meaning of divine encounter with a different referent, even though this meaning is acknowledged as a valid secondary sense in prophetic literature.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The book's definition ignores dominant biblical occurrences where mountains represent theophanies, worship sites, or places of spiritual encounter (such as Sinai and the Mount of Transfiguration), artificially restricting a polyvalent symbol to its political and governmental sense.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
